PROJECT 3: STUDENT PERFORMANCE VISUALIZATION USING MATPLOTLIB

==================================================
1. PROJECT OBJECTIVE
==================================================

The objective is to visualize student marks using Python and Matplotlib. The project creates bar, line, and pie charts and calculates summary statistics.

2. TECHNOLOGIES USED
- Python
- Jupyter Notebook
- Anaconda
- Matplotlib

3. LIBRARY IMPORT

import matplotlib.pyplot as plt

Here:
- matplotlib is the plotting library.
- pyplot is a module that provides plotting functions.
- plt is a commonly used alias for pyplot.

4. DATA USED

subjects = ["Maths", "Science", "English", "Computer", "Hindi"]
marks = [85, 78, 92, 95, 80]

The subjects list contains category names. The marks list contains the corresponding scores.

5. TYPES OF CHARTS

A. Bar chart:
Uses rectangular bars to compare marks across subjects.
Function: plt.bar()

B. Line chart:
Connects data points to show changes in values.
Function: plt.plot()

C. Pie chart:
Shows each subject's share of the total marks.
Function: plt.pie()

D. Horizontal bar chart:
Displays horizontal bars.
Function: plt.barh()

E. Scatter plot:
Displays the relationship between two numerical variables.
Function: plt.scatter()

F. Histogram:
Displays the distribution of numerical data.
Function: plt.hist()

6. SUMMARY STATISTICS

total = sum(marks)
average = total / len(marks)
highest = max(marks)
lowest = min(marks)

Expected results:
Total = 430 / 500
Average = 86
Highest mark = 95
Lowest mark = 78

7. MATPLOTLIB FUNCTIONS AND METHODS

A. FIGURE AND AXES
- plt.figure(): Creates a figure.
- plt.subplots(): Creates a figure and one or more axes.
- plt.subplot(): Selects a subplot in a grid.
- plt.gca(): Gets the current Axes.
- plt.gcf(): Gets the current Figure.
- plt.axes(): Adds or selects axes.
- plt.sca(): Sets the current Axes.
- plt.clf(): Clears the current figure.
- plt.cla(): Clears the current axes.
- plt.close(): Closes a figure.

B. BASIC PLOTTING
- plt.plot(): Creates line plots.
- plt.scatter(): Creates scatter plots.
- plt.bar(): Creates vertical bar charts.
- plt.barh(): Creates horizontal bar charts.
- plt.hist(): Creates histograms.
- plt.pie(): Creates pie charts.
- plt.boxplot(): Creates box plots.
- plt.violinplot(): Creates violin plots.
- plt.stem(): Creates stem plots.
- plt.step(): Creates step plots.
- plt.stackplot(): Creates stacked area plots.
- plt.fill_between(): Fills the area between curves.
- plt.errorbar(): Displays data with error bars.
- plt.hexbin(): Creates hexagonal binning plots.
- plt.eventplot(): Displays event data.
- plt.imshow(): Displays image or 2D array data.
- plt.matshow(): Displays a matrix as an image.
- plt.contour(): Creates contour lines.
- plt.contourf(): Creates filled contour plots.
- plt.pcolor(): Creates pseudocolor plots.
- plt.pcolormesh(): Creates a pseudocolor mesh.
- plt.spy(): Displays the sparsity pattern of a matrix.

C. TITLES AND LABELS
- plt.title(): Sets the chart title.
- plt.xlabel(): Sets the x-axis label.
- plt.ylabel(): Sets the y-axis label.
- plt.suptitle(): Adds a figure-level title.
- plt.text(): Adds text at specified coordinates.
- plt.annotate(): Adds an annotation with optional arrow.
- plt.figtext(): Adds text at figure coordinates.

D. AXES AND TICKS
- plt.xlim(): Sets x-axis limits.
- plt.ylim(): Sets y-axis limits.
- plt.xticks(): Sets or gets x-axis tick positions and labels.
- plt.yticks(): Sets or gets y-axis tick positions and labels.
- plt.tick_params(): Customizes tick marks and labels.
- plt.minorticks_on(): Enables minor ticks.
- plt.minorticks_off(): Disables minor ticks.
- plt.margins(): Sets margins around plotted data.
- plt.axis(): Gets or sets axis properties.
- plt.gca().invert_xaxis(): Reverses the x-axis.
- plt.gca().invert_yaxis(): Reverses the y-axis.

E. GRID AND LEGEND
- plt.grid(): Displays or customizes grid lines.
- plt.legend(): Displays the legend.
- plt.legend(loc="best"): Places the legend automatically.
- plt.colorbar(): Adds a color scale to a plot.

F. APPEARANCE AND STYLING
- plt.style.use(): Applies a predefined style.
- plt.set_cmap(): Sets the default colormap.
- plt.get_cmap(): Retrieves a colormap.
- plt.tight_layout(): Adjusts spacing to prevent overlap.
- plt.subplots_adjust(): Adjusts subplot spacing.
- plt.rcParams: Controls default plotting settings.
- plt.margins(): Adjusts plot margins.

G. LINES, PATCHES, AND SHAPES
- plt.axhline(): Adds a horizontal reference line.
- plt.axvline(): Adds a vertical reference line.
- plt.axline(): Adds a line through a point.
- plt.hlines(): Adds horizontal line segments.
- plt.vlines(): Adds vertical line segments.
- plt.fill(): Draws and fills a polygon.
- plt.arrow(): Draws an arrow.
- plt.axhspan(): Highlights a horizontal band.
- plt.axvspan(): Highlights a vertical band.

H. FIGURE DISPLAY AND EXPORT
- plt.show(): Displays the figure.
- plt.savefig(): Saves a figure to a file.
- plt.pause(): Pauses briefly and updates the figure.
- plt.close(): Closes the figure.

I. USEFUL AXES METHODS
These are methods called on an Axes object, commonly named ax:
- ax.plot(): Creates a line plot.
- ax.scatter(): Creates a scatter plot.
- ax.bar(): Creates a bar chart.
- ax.barh(): Creates a horizontal bar chart.
- ax.hist(): Creates a histogram.
- ax.pie(): Creates a pie chart.
- ax.set_title(): Sets the axes title.
- ax.set_xlabel(): Sets the x-axis label.
- ax.set_ylabel(): Sets the y-axis label.
- ax.set_xlim(): Sets x-axis limits.
- ax.set_ylim(): Sets y-axis limits.
- ax.set_xticks(): Sets x-axis tick positions.
- ax.set_yticks(): Sets y-axis tick positions.
- ax.legend(): Adds a legend.
- ax.grid(): Adds grid lines.
- ax.text(): Adds text.
- ax.annotate(): Adds annotations.
- ax.set_aspect(): Controls the aspect ratio.
- ax.set_axis_off(): Hides the axes.
- ax.twinx(): Creates a second y-axis.
- ax.twiny(): Creates a second x-axis.

8. IMPORTANT PYTHON FUNCTIONS USED

- print(): Displays output.
- sum(): Calculates the sum of marks.
- len(): Counts the number of items.
- max(): Finds the highest value.
- min(): Finds the lowest value.

9. IMPORTANT PARAMETERS

- figsize=(8, 5): Sets figure width and height in inches.
- color: Sets the plot color.
- marker="o": Adds circular markers.
- linestyle="--": Sets a dashed line.
- linewidth: Sets line thickness.
- alpha: Controls transparency.
- label: Names a data series for the legend.
- bins: Sets the number of histogram bins.
- autopct="%1.1f%%": Displays pie percentages.
- startangle=90: Rotates the pie chart.
- dpi=300: Sets image resolution when saving.
- bbox_inches="tight": Reduces excess whitespace around saved figures.

10. EXAMPLE CODE

import matplotlib.pyplot as plt

subjects = ["Maths", "Science", "English", "Computer", "Hindi"]
marks = [85, 78, 92, 95, 80]

plt.figure(figsize=(8, 5))
plt.bar(subjects, marks)
plt.title("Student Marks by Subject")
plt.xlabel("Subjects")
plt.ylabel("Marks out of 100")
plt.ylim(0, 100)
plt.grid(axis="y", linestyle="--", alpha=0.4)
plt.tight_layout()
plt.savefig("student_marks_bar_chart.png", dpi=300, bbox_inches="tight")
plt.show()

11. HOW TO RUN

1. Open the notebook in Jupyter Notebook.
2. Ensure Matplotlib is installed.
3. Run each code cell with Shift + Enter.
4. Observe the bar, line, and pie charts.
5. Save the notebook with Ctrl + S.

12. INSTALLATION

If Matplotlib is missing, run this command in Anaconda Prompt:

conda install matplotlib

Restart the notebook kernel after installation if necessary.

13. LEARNING OUTCOMES

- Learned to import and use Matplotlib.
- Learned to create bar, line, and pie charts.
- Learned to add titles, labels, legends, and grids.
- Learned to customize axes and chart appearance.
- Learned to calculate summary statistics.
- Learned to save plots as image files.

14. LIMITATIONS AND FUTURE IMPROVEMENTS

Limitations:
- The project uses a small, fixed sample dataset.
- The line chart connects subjects in an arbitrary order.
- The pie chart represents each mark's share of the total, not the percentage score out of 100.

Future improvements:
- Read student data from a CSV file using pandas.
- Compare multiple students.
- Create histograms and scatter plots.
- Save multiple charts automatically.
- Apply data visualization to agriculture, rainfall, and NDVI datasets.

